<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px 0; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Arts and Crafts</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is a Historical Building.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze simple editorials or opinion pieces. Reading: Create visual presentations summarizing informational texts. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by applying knowledge of affixes to base words in context.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze simple editorials or opinion pieces related to arts and crafts, identifying key themes and viewpoints (e.g., "What is the cultural significance of this piece of art").
            Can create a drawing or a model of a famous historical building based on a text description (e.g., "The Biomuseo is a colorful building with a unique design by Frank Gehry").
            Can decode and accurately pronounce words by applying knowledge of affixes to base words in context, such as past tense verbs and gerunds, enhancing phonemic awareness (e.g., played, playing).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of famous historical buildings in Panama (e.g., Biomuseo, Panama Canal Administration Building, Casco Antiguo architecture) and examples of traditional Panamanian crafts like molas, tagua carvings, and woven baskets. Students discuss in pairs which images they recognize and what makes them unique or special. Teacher asks: "What do these buildings and crafts have in common? How are they artistic?" This activates prior knowledge and vocabulary (building, unique, colorful, artistic, craft, design) from Lesson 1.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces the concept of analyzing a text by showing a short, simple paragraph on the board about the Biomuseo's design. The teacher reads it aloud, highlighting how to identify key words (e.g., "Frank Gehry," "colorful," "unique design"). The teacher models the pronunciation of "sculpture" (/skʌlp.tʃər/) and "design" (/dɪ'zaɪn/), emphasizing syllable stress. The teacher also highlights words with -ed (e.g., "designed," "created") and -ing (e.g., "building," "creating") endings, modeling their articulation for phonemic awareness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If we read an article about a building, what kind of information do we look for first?" (Expected answer: Main idea, name, location, important details.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why is it important to understand the main idea of a text?" (Expected answer: To know what the text is about, to summarize it.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Arts and Crafts".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short informational text (approx. 100-120 words) or simple editorial titled "The Biomuseo: A Masterpiece of Art and Science" describing the building's design, architect Frank Gehry, and its unique features, mentioning its colorful panels and the concept behind its creation. The text includes target vocabulary (building, design, colorful, unique, artist, create, exhibition) and grammatical structures (Present perfect: "Frank Gehry has created...", Present simple: "The Biomuseo is...", Past simple: "He designed...", Imperatives related to appreciating art: "Look at the vibrant colors...").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Analyze simple informational texts related to arts and crafts, identifying key themes and descriptive details of historical buildings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the text silently to get the main idea. Teacher asks: "What is the main topic of this article? Is it about a person, a building, or an animal?" (Answer: A building.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students read sentences based on the text and mark them True or False (e.g., "The Biomuseo is a boring building. T/F," "Frank Gehry designed the building. T/F," "The building has colorful panels. T/F").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match key vocabulary words from the text (e.g., "architect," "exhibition," "sculpture") with their simple definitions or synonyms, reinforcing understanding for A2.2 level.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice identifying key details, opinions, and grammatical structures within the text. Focus on Present perfect for achievements, Present/Past simple for descriptions, Imperatives for appreciating features, and target vocabulary (artist, building, design, create, colorful, unique, detailed). Phonemic awareness will be reinforced by identifying and articulating words with -ed and -ing endings in context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Cloze & Grammar Identification):</b> Students receive the text again, but this time with certain words (target vocabulary or verbs) removed. They fill in the blanks, choosing from a word bank. Additionally, they identify and underline all verbs in the present perfect, present simple, and past simple, and circle all words ending in -ed or -ing. (e.g., "The Biomuseo ___ (is/was) a colorful building. Frank Gehry ___ (has designed/designed) it.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Completion & Detail Extraction):</b> Students read specific sections of the text to answer detail questions or complete sentences. For example: "The Biomuseo is famous for its ___ design and ___ colors." Or "Frank Gehry is an architect who has ___ many ___ buildings." (emphasizing Present Perfect and adjectives). Students also identify sentences that describe how to appreciate the building (imperatives), e.g., "Observe the different angles."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Peer Check & Discussion):</b> In pairs, students compare their answers from the previous activities, discussing any discrepancies and referring back to the text to justify their choices. They also discuss what they consider the "most unique" or "most artistic" feature of the Biomuseo according to the text. Teacher monitors, providing feedback on comprehension and pronunciation of words with inflectional endings.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Arts and Crafts".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Drawing/Model Presentation. Students work in small groups (3-4) to create a drawing or a simple model (using paper, clay, or other available materials) that visually summarizes the key features of the Biomuseo as described in the informational text. This task directly addresses the learning outcome "Can create a drawing or a model of a famous historical building based on a text description."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Each group must clearly represent elements like the colorful panels, the unique design by Frank Gehry, and any other notable features from the reading. They will briefly present their creation, explaining which parts of the text inspired their design using descriptive language (e.g., "Our drawing shows a two-story structure, with a rectangular design and very vibrant colors. The artist has created a bold exhibition for nature.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> "This Is a Historical Building." The Biomuseo is a modern historical building. The act of creating a drawing/model is an "Arts and Crafts" activity, linking the scenario.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in comprehending the text and translating it into a visual representation. Collaborative interaction within the group to interpret the text and complete the task. Communicative task completion is successful if the drawing/model accurately reflects the textual description.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher uses a checklist to evaluate each group's drawing/model and their brief explanation based on clarity, accuracy of textual representation, and use of target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their drawing or model to the class. During the presentation, they must orally describe their creation, stating how it reflects the information read in the article about the Biomuseo. They should point out specific features (e.g., "Here is the colorful roof, designed by the artist Frank Gehry," "This part shows its unique structure.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (on checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Accuracy of Visual Representation:</b> Does the drawing/model accurately summarize the building's features as described in the text? (Reading comprehension)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Explanation:</b> Can students clearly explain how their visual connects to the text? (Reading summary)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Do students use words like 'building,' 'design,' 'colorful,' 'unique,' 'artist,' 'create' during their explanation?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Is pronunciation of key vocabulary and words with -ed/-ing endings clear?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Did all group members contribute to the task?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting fact you read about the Biomuseo?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How easy or difficult was it to create a drawing based only on the text description?" (1=very easy, 5=very difficult)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategy helped you the most today to understand the text about the Biomuseo?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can reading descriptions help you understand and appreciate art or buildings better?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The reading comprehension activities and the visual presentation task effectively engaged students in analyzing the text. The target vocabulary was reinforced through contextual use, and the focus on phonemic awareness with -ed/-ing endings was integrated into modeling and practice. Some students might need additional support in identifying subjective viewpoints in simple editorials (though today's was informational). For next time, I could provide more visual scaffolding for less common vocabulary if a more complex text is used. The group drawing/model worked well as a demonstration of reading comprehension.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the knowledge gained from reading and the visual representations they created to engage in discussions and express their opinions about historical buildings and art, practicing describing creations and achievements of artists using the present perfect and descriptive adjectives.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>